<main class="lux-article-page"><nav class="lux-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en', '')); ?>">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Knowledge Blog</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Fresh Truffle, Truffle Sauce and Truffle-Flavoured Oil: What Should You Look for When Buying?</span></nav><article class="lux-reader-layout lux-academy-reader">
    <div class="lux-reader-rule"></div>
    <section class="lux-reader-hero"><div class="lux-reader-hero-copy"><div class="lux-reader-meta-grid"><span>Truffle Academy</span><span>Truffle Academy · 2026</span></div><h1 id="lux-reader-title">Fresh Truffle, Truffle Sauce and Truffle-Flavoured Oil: What Should You Look for When Buying?</h1><p class="lux-reader-summary">The world of truffle products is much broader than fresh truffle. An Italian food shop may offer fresh truffle, truffle sauces, creamy truffle spreads, truffle-flavoured oils, truffle salt, truffle honey, cheeses, condiments, and many other processed products.</p></div><figure class="lux-reader-cover"><img width="2000" height="1500" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/truffle-buying-truffle-products.webp'); ?>" alt="Fresh Truffle, Truffle Sauce and Truffle-Flavoured Oil: What Should You Look for When Buying?"></figure></section>
    <section class="lux-reader-content"><aside class="lux-reader-aside"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Back to Knowledge Blog</a></aside><div class="lux-reader-copy"><section class="lux-reader-section lux-reader-section-opening"><p>All may be connected with truffle.</p><p>But they are clearly not the same product.</p><p>The useful question is therefore not:</p><p>“Is this a truffle product?”</p><p>It is:</p><p>“What kind of truffle product is this?”</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-0"><h2>Buying Fresh Truffle: Start With the Species</h2><p>Fresh truffle should first be identified by species.</p><p>Precious white truffle, precious black truffle, summer truffle, and other edible truffles are different products.</p><p>They should not simply be reduced to “white” and “black.”</p><p>Consumers do not need to memorise every Latin botanical name.</p><p>But they should ideally understand:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>which truffle they are buying;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>whether it is in season;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>how it is intended to be used.</li></ul></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-1"><h2>Buying Truffle Sauce: Read Beyond the Front Label</h2><p>There is no single universal recipe for “truffle sauce.”</p><p>Some products use mushrooms, olives, oils, dairy ingredients, or other foods as a base and then incorporate truffle.</p><p>Others use different percentages or different truffle species.</p><p>The ingredient list therefore matters.</p><p>Useful questions include:</p><p>1. Which truffle species is used? 2. What percentage of truffle is present when declared? 3. What are the main supporting ingredients? 4. Are there important allergens such as dairy or nuts? 5. How is the product intended to be used? 6. How should it be stored after opening?</p><p>This information is often much more useful than simply seeing the word “truffle” printed prominently on the front of the package.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-2"><h2>Buying Truffle-Flavoured Oil: Understand the Product Category</h2><p>Truffle-flavoured oil should be understood as a seasoning product.</p><p>It is not simply “fresh truffle transformed into liquid.”</p><p>Consumers can look at:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>the type of base oil;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>how the ingredient list describes the truffle and aromatic components;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>the intensity of the flavour;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>recommended serving quantity;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>whether the product is intended primarily for finishing or cooking.</li></ul><p>With strongly aromatic products, starting with a small quantity often provides better control.</p><p>A few drops can be added first, followed by more only if necessary.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-3"><h2>Do Not Judge Every Truffle Product Only by Percentage</h2><p>Truffle percentage can be useful information.</p><p>But it is not the only information that matters.</p><p>Different products are created for different functions.</p><p>One sauce may be designed for pasta.</p><p>Another may work as a spread.</p><p>A truffle-flavoured oil may be intended as a finishing condiment.</p><p>Truffle salt may be designed for quick everyday seasoning.</p><p>“Highest truffle percentage” does not automatically mean “best product for my purpose.”</p><p>A more useful sequence is:</p><p>product type → truffle species → ingredient composition → flavour profile → intended use → storage.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-4"><h2>Good Product Information Should Reduce Guesswork</h2><p>Consumers should not have to guess what a food product contains or how it should be used.</p><p>If a truffle product communicates only ideas such as “Italian,” “luxury,” “premium,” and “selected,” but does not explain what it contains or how it works in the kitchen, important information is still missing.</p><p>Transparency does not make a premium product less premium.</p><p>On the contrary, clear information about ingredients and use can support long-term trust.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-5"><h2>LuxurEat(露意膳) Perspective</h2><p>This is also the approach LuxurEat(露意膳) aims to develop for its truffle range.</p><p>Instead of simply telling consumers that something is a “premium Italian truffle product,” the product presentation should make practical information visible:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>the truffle species;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>major ingredients;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>recommended uses;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>storage instructions;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>related Italian recipes.</li></ul><p>The LuxurEat(露意膳) Truffle Academy and the product catalogue should therefore support each other.</p><p>The Academy helps consumers understand truffle.</p><p>Recipes explain how to use it.</p><p>Products transform that knowledge into something that can actually enter the kitchen.</p><p>The final path becomes:</p><p>discover truffle → understand the differences → learn how to pair it → choose the right product → use it with confidence.</p></section></div></section>
